@page "/docs"
@layout DocsLayout
@using MudBlazor

<PageTitle>Docs — AgentBlazor</PageTitle>

<div class="docs-article">
    <section class="docs-hero">
        <p class="docs-hero__eyebrow">Documentation</p>
        <h1 class="docs-hero__title">A complete install, verification, and authoring guide for real Blazor apps.</h1>
        <p class="docs-hero__lede">
            AgentBlazor is a Blazor-native workflow runtime with in-app chat surfaces, deterministic component actions, approvals,
            and route-scoped workflow registration. These docs are written as a runbook: what to install, which commands to run, how to verify the result,
            and where the supported host paths stop being automatic.
        </p>

        <div class="docs-actions">
            <MudButton Variant="Variant.Filled" Color="Color.Primary" Href="/docs/getting-started">Getting Started</MudButton>
            <MudButton Variant="Variant.Outlined" Color="Color.Secondary" Href="/docs/cli">Open CLI Guide</MudButton>
            <MudButton Variant="Variant.Outlined" Color="Color.Secondary" Href="/demo/workflows/response-orchestration?reset=true">Open Live Demo</MudButton>
        </div>

        <div class="docs-chip-row">
            <span>OpenAI is the most validated provider path</span>
            <span>CLI is preview-first before apply</span>
            <span>Hosted WebAssembly remote chat has a dedicated client path</span>
            <span>Advanced hosts stay review-first when patching is risky</span>
        </div>

        <div class="docs-kpi-grid">
            <div class="docs-kpi">
                <strong>Canonical flow</strong>
                <span><code>init</code> -> <code>scaffold --diff</code> -> <code>--approve</code> -> <code>restore/build</code> -> <code>doctor</code> -> <code>validate</code></span>
            </div>
            <div class="docs-kpi">
                <strong>3 provider flags</strong>
                <span><code>openai</code>, <code>azure-openai</code>, <code>ollama</code></span>
            </div>
            <div class="docs-kpi">
                <strong>Verification first</strong>
                <span>Placeholder-key runtime smoke plus domain prompt checks before live-provider testing</span>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="docs-section">
        <div class="docs-section__header">
            <div>
                <h2>Start here if you are onboarding an app</h2>
                <p>Most developers need four questions answered up front.</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="docs-card-grid">
            <div class="docs-card">
                <strong>Which package source should I use?</strong>
                <p>The current preview path is GitHub Packages. Pin exact versions for the runtime, CLI, and hosted WebAssembly client package.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="docs-card">
                <strong>What exact commands do I run?</strong>
                <p>Use <code>init</code>, preview with <code>scaffold --diff</code>, apply with <code>--approve</code>, then run restore/build, <code>doctor</code>, and <code>validate</code>.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="docs-card">
                <strong>How do I know it really worked?</strong>
                <p>Run a placeholder-key startup smoke and verify a chat surface can answer prompts that reflect the real workflow on the page.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="docs-card">
                <strong>When does the CLI stay conservative?</strong>
                <p>When the host shape is advanced, legacy, or otherwise risky to patch automatically. Review-first is a safety decision, not a missing feature.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="docs-section">
        <div class="docs-section__header">
            <div>
                <h2>Recommended run order</h2>
                <p>This is the command path the rest of the docs assume.</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <pre class="docs-code"><code>dotnet tool install --global AgentBlazor.Cli --version 0.1.0-preview.9 --add-source https://nuget.pkg.github.com/ashpeterson/index.json
agentblazor init ./MySolution.slnx --host MyBlazorApp
agentblazor scaffold ./MySolution.slnx --host MyBlazorApp --provider openai --diff
agentblazor scaffold ./MySolution.slnx --host MyBlazorApp --provider openai --approve
dotnet restore ./MySolution.slnx --force-evaluate
dotnet build ./MySolution.slnx --no-restore -nologo
agentblazor doctor ./MySolution.slnx --host MyBlazorApp
agentblazor validate ./MySolution.slnx --host MyBlazorApp</code></pre>

        <div class="docs-note">
            <strong>Keep versions aligned:</strong> if you are testing the preview feed, pin the same AgentBlazor version for the runtime package,
            <code>AgentBlazor.Client</code> when needed, and <code>AgentBlazor.Cli</code>.
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="docs-section">
        <div class="docs-section__header">
            <div>
                <h2>Choose the right install path</h2>
                <p>The docs are split by decision, not by marketing copy.</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="docs-card-grid">
            <div class="docs-card">
                <strong>CLI-assisted install</strong>
                <p>Fastest path for existing Blazor apps. Use when you want preview-first file planning, exact diffs, and guided host-shape behavior.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="docs-card">
                <strong>Manual runtime install</strong>
                <p>Best when you want to understand every moving part directly: package, services, shell assets, endpoint mapping, one workflow, one chat surface.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="docs-card">
                <strong>Hosted WebAssembly</strong>
                <p>Use the server host for CLI/runtime wiring, then mount <code>AgentBlazor.Client</code> remote chat components in the client project.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="docs-card">
                <strong>Review-first custom hosts</strong>
                <p>Legacy server and custom framework hosts still get safe file additions, but risky shell or startup edits remain explicit manual review.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="docs-section">
        <div class="docs-section__header">
            <div>
                <h2>What is validated today</h2>
                <p>The docs should make the current boundary clear so developers know what to trust first.</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="docs-card-grid">
            <div class="docs-card">
                <strong>Most validated provider</strong>
                <p>OpenAI via <code>UseOpenAI(...)</code> remains the primary path for setup, workflow invocation, approvals, and browser prompt validation.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="docs-card">
                <strong>Standard host path</strong>
                <p>Standard Blazor Web App hosts are the cleanest scaffold/apply path and the best place to start when evaluating AgentBlazor.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="docs-card">
                <strong>Hosted WebAssembly</strong>
                <p>The supported client path is remote chat over <code>MapAgentBlazorRemoteChat()</code> with <code>AgentBlazor.Client</code> in the browser project.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="docs-card">
                <strong>Verification expectation</strong>
                <p>Do not stop at build success. The runtime, assets, endpoint, and prompts all need to be verified on the page.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="docs-section">
        <div class="docs-section__header">
            <div>
                <h2>Docs map</h2>
